course: dictionary
lesson: 7161
title: Many meanings of period
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word period has several meanings, so the words around it tell you which one you need. It can be a length of time, a school lesson, a time in history, or a dot at the end of a sentence. Always ask: what does the sentence say about time, school, history or writing?
explain: «The Renaissance was a period of new learning and art in Europe from around 1400 to 1600.» The years 1400 to 1600 are a long stretch of time, so period means an age in history. Swap in a time in history and the sentence still makes sense.
explain: «Furloughed» means sent home from work for a period, often unpaid. Here period means a length of time, not a lesson or a dot. Try the swap: "for a length of time" fits, so you have the right meaning.
explain: In British English the dot at the end of a sentence is a full stop. In American English the same mark is called a period. The mark is the same, but the name changes, so check where the text was written.
fact: A period can be a length of time, such as «over a period of three hours».
fact: At school, a period is one lesson in the timetable.
fact: In history, a period is an age, like the Tudor period or the Stone Age.
fact: In American English, a period is the dot (.) at the end of a sentence; British English calls it a full stop.
fact: In sport, some matches, like ice hockey, are divided into periods.
exercise: «The Renaissance was a period of new learning and art in Europe.» In this sentence, period means: | A dot at the end of a sentence | *A time in history | A school lesson | A type of pen
exercise: A maths problem says, «The tap ran over a period of three hours.» What does period mean? | *A length of time | A time in history | A row in a table of science | A school lesson
exercise: An American teacher says, «Put a period at the end.» What mark does she mean? | *A full stop (.) | A comma (,) | An exclamation mark (!) | A question mark (?)
test: «Mum was furloughed for a period of six months.» What does period mean here? | A school lesson | *A length of time | A time in history | A dot at the end of a sentence
test: «We have maths in the second period after lunch.» What does period mean? | A time in history | A row in the periodic table | A dot at the end of a sentence | *A school lesson
exam: «Ethiopia kept its independence through the colonial period, apart from an Italian occupation from 1936 to 1941.» What does period mean here? | A length of ten minutes | A dot at the end of a sentence | A school lesson | *A time in history
